// BuiltinConformanceRuntime.maxon — the synthesized `int.hash`/`int.equals` (P1.7a slice 2b),
// `int.compare` (P1.7a slice 2b-ii), `float.equals`/`float.compare` + `bool.compare` (P1.7a slice 2b-iii),
// `String.hash`/`String.equals` (P1.7b) and `float.hash` (P1.7a's last slice) impls that make the BUILTIN
// types conform to `Hashable`/`Equatable`/`Comparable`. WHICH type conforms to WHICH protocol is not
// restated here — it is
// `IrInterface.isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance`, and the impls below are exactly its true entries — one per
// (type, protocol) pair, no count written down, because a count is a copy that goes stale on the next rung.
//
// ⭐ **The builtin types conform NATIVELY, the type-side twin of the synthesized
// `Hashable`/`Equatable`/`Comparable` interfaces (`builtinInterface`).** shv2 reads no stdlib, so there is no
// user `int.hash` / `float.compare` / `String.equals` to point a witness slot at — these are hand-built Std
// bodies, exactly like `__str_eq` / the managed-memory runtime, installed after the pipeline so they are never
// re-optimized. They are NOT `__`-prefixed, so they carry a real `.text` symbol the witness-table reloc
// (`recordFuncAbs64InRdata`) can name — the SAME funcAbs64 reloc that stamps `__witness_<Struct>.Digest`, only
// here the target is synthesized rather than user-written. (`String` is a MANAGED concrete type, not a
// primitive — hence the neutral "builtin" spelling over the older "primitive".)
//
// ⭐ **INSTALL IS DRIVEN BY THE REFERENCE, NOT BY A SEPARATE SCAN.** The install asks the emitted program
// one question — *"does anything NAME this impl?"* — and builds the impl iff the answer is yes.
// **REFERENCED ⟺ INSTALLED**, with no second predicate to drift from the first.
//
// There are exactly TWO ways a program can name one, and the question covers both because both are read off
// what was actually emitted rather than off a parallel record of intent:
//
//   • **A WITNESS-TABLE SLOT.** A slot of `__witness_int.Hashable` / `__witness_String.Equatable` / … is
//     stamped with a `funcAbs64` reloc naming `int.hash` / `String.equals` / … iff the program dispatches
//     through it (`ensureWitnessTable`, during lowering). The reference is the RELOC.
//   • **A DIRECT DISPATCH.** `i.compare(j)` on a concrete value is an ordinary `call int.compare`
//     (`Parser.emitBuiltinConformanceCall`) and creates NO reloc at all — the witness table it would have
//     ridden is never built. The reference is the CALL.
//
// ⚠ The reloc half alone was the whole predicate until direct dispatch shipped, and *"no pending reloc ⇒
// return early"* then silently declined to install the very impl a direct call had just named — a link-time
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function 'int.compare'`. The invariant that survives both is
// REFERENCED, not table-built; the shape it is asked in — one walk of the finished module — is what makes a
// third kind of reference impossible to forget, because a reference is by definition something the module
// spells.
//
// Golden-neutral for every program that names no impl (the conformance-free corpus): the walk builds
// nothing, and `eliminateDeadFunctions` would prune an over-installed impl anyway.
//
// The SEMANTICS match the reference compilers' monomorphizing source (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon`,
// `stdlib/helpers/string/hash.maxon`): `int.hash() = self & 0xFFFFFFFF` (the low-32 mask — identity for a
// value that already fits u32); `float.hash() = bitcast(self) & 0xFFFFFFFF`, whose mask ALSO normalizes
// `-0.0` — see `buildFloatHash`, which is where shv2 diverges from both references by omitting a branch
// they write and that mask makes dead; `<scalar>.equals(other) = self == other`; `<scalar>.compare(other)` = the
// `lessThan`/`greaterThan`/`equalTo` cascade, which `float.compare` precedes with a NaN preamble
// (`buildFloatCompare`); `String.hash() = djb2` over the bytes; `String.equals(other) = __str_eq`'s content
// compare. The witness reloc these ride is baked by EVERY writer: the x64 one, the wasm one as a
// funcref-table index (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B) and the arm64 Mach-O/ELF one (slice 2b-iv-C).
//
// ⛔⛔ **FIVE OF THE FOURTEEN CANDIDATES CAN NO LONGER BE INSTALLED IN ANY COMPILE, AND THAT IS THE
// "does not already DEFINE it" HALF WORKING RATHER THAN A HOLE (MEASURED, W49 wave 7).**
// `String.hash`/`String.equals` and `Character.hash`/`Character.equals`/`Character.compare` are all
// DECLARED by corpus modules the loader lists UNCONDITIONALLY (`stdlib/String.maxon:351`, `:355`;
// `stdlib/Character.maxon:107`, `:62`, `:80`), and `LowerMaxonToStd` pushes every stdlib function into
// `module.functions` even when it skips the BODY — so `undefinedImplNames` finds each of the five DEFINED in
// every compile and never offers it here.
//
// **Measured, not inferred, on the base binary before a line of wave 7 was written:** `s.hash()` on a
// `String` emits `call String.hash`, and the emitted `String.hash` is `call hashString` — the corpus's
// `helpers/string/hash.maxon:22`. A `Set with String` reaches the same body through its witness slot, and a
// `Set with Character` emits a `Character.hash` that calls `__str_bytes_view`/`__managed_count` — the corpus's
// `bytes()` walk, not the djb2 above. Three doors, one body, and the body is the CORPUS's.
//
// ⚠⚠ **DO NOT PRUNE THE FIVE.** The candidate list is DERIVED from `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` — one
// entry per (type, protocol) pair it admits — and which of them a corpus module happens to define is a fact
// owned by `StdlibLoader.whitelistedStdlibModules`. Deleting entries here would be that whitelist written a
// SECOND time, in a file with no test between it and the original: unlist a module and the entries would
// have to come back, silently, or the program dies at link time naming a symbol whose builder was deleted.
// The runtime filter already answers the question exactly, per compile, and costs one walk.
//
// ⇒ The two hashes AGREE where it would matter if they did not: `hashString` is the same djb2 with the same
// `5381`/`33`/low-32 mask as `emitDjb2HashOverByteRange`, so a `Set with String`'s bucket layout would be
// identical whichever body a compile ended up with. `Character`'s pair do NOT agree on cost (the corpus
// walks a materialized `bytes()` view where the synthesized one reads `buffer@0` directly), which is a
// trade belonging to whichever rung retires `Character.bytes()`, and is recorded at `ByteViewMethod`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THAT AGREEMENT IS A READING OF TWO SOURCES AND IS CHECKED BY NOTHING — DO NOT PROMOTE IT TO A
// GUARANTEE.** It cannot be tested, and the reason is the very fact above: the synthesized `String.hash`
// is unreachable in every compile, so no program can be made to run it and disagree. That is not a hole to
// plug with a test — a case that could witness the divergence would first have to make the impossible
// branch happen. What it means practically is narrower and safer than "they agree": **only the corpus body
// ever runs**, so a `Set with String`'s bucket layout depends on `hashString` ALONE, and the djb2 here is
// documentation of a shape rather than a second implementation anything compares against. ⇒ The day a
// conformer's declaring module stops being listed, this arm becomes live and the agreement becomes a real
// obligation — and there would still be no test. Read both bodies then.

// The low-32-bit mask EVERY builtin hasher folds its result through — `HashValue` is `int(0 to u32.max)`,
// so a hash is the low 32 bits. The one place the mask is spelled (it IS `HashValue`'s width, one fact);
// `returnLow32Masked` is the one place the FOLD is spelled, and the roster of hashers lives there rather
// than here, so this constant carries no count to go stale.
//
// ⚠ For `float.hash` this mask is LOAD-BEARING rather than incidental: `buildFloatHash` omits the `-0.0`
// branch both references write purely because a 32-bit mask discards IEEE-754's sign bit. That dependence
// is checked, not remembered — see `assertSignBitAboveHashMask`.
let HashLow32Mask = 0xFFFFFFFF

// ⭐ **`-0.0`'s IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN.** `+0.0` is 64 zero bits and `-0.0` is those bits with the SIGN
// BIT set, so the pattern IS the sign bit alone: `0x8000000000000000`. It is spelled `i64.min` because
// `ParsedInt` is SIGNED and that is the number Maxon can write for the top bit (v1 writes the same
// value as `-9223372036854775807 - 1`, the positive-literal parse being one short of it).
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS READ BY A CHECK, NOT BY THE HASH — see `assertSignBitAboveHashMask`.** The two zeros
// must hash alike (`-0.0 == 0.0` is true under IEEE, so `float.equals` calls them equal and `Hashable`
// then demands one hash), and BOTH reference compilers buy that with an explicit `if bits == -0.0
// return 0` branch. **shv2 does not, because under a low-32 mask that branch is DEAD**: the sign bit
// is bit 63 and the mask keeps bits 0–31, so `-0.0` masks to 0 exactly as `+0.0` does, and the branch
// returns what the fallthrough already returns. That is not special to zero — it is why `-3.14` and
// `3.14` hash alike too (`primitive-hashable/float.hash.negative`). The constant's job here is to make
// the DEPENDENCE on the mask's width checkable instead of remembered.
//
// ⚠ It is the same bit `Parser.FloatSignBit` XORs to negate a float literal, and the two are NOT one
// constant: that one is a MASK the front end applies to a literal, this one is a VALUE the check reads.
// Merging them would make the parser export a numeric constant down into the runtime layer, and
// `Arm64Backend.Arm64WordSignBit` is a third same-valued constant that declines the same merge for the
// same reason.
let NegativeZeroBits = i64.min as ParsedInt

// djb2 over `String`'s bytes: `h = Djb2Seed`, then `h = h * Djb2Multiplier + b` per byte. The two magic
// numbers of the algorithm, named rather than bare so the loop reads as djb2. `""` -> `5381`, `"abc"` ->
// `193485963`, `"hi"` -> `5863446`.
let Djb2Seed = 5381
let Djb2Multiplier = 33

// ⭐ **THE WITNESS-TABLE SLOT OF A BUILTIN PROTOCOL'S SOLE METHOD.** `Hashable` declares only `hash`,
// `Equatable` only `equals` and `Comparable` only `compare` (`singleMethodInterface`), so each sits at
// decl-order index 0 and a fused `witnessCall` reads its function pointer from `[witnessAddr + 24]`
// (`witnessMethodSlotOffset(0)`).
//
// ⚠ It lives HERE, beside the impls those slots point at, rather than in any one CONSUMER's file: it
// was the deleted `HashTableRuntime`'s `HtWitnessMethodIndex` while a hash table was the only runtime that dispatched
// one, and the `Array` surface's `contains` is the second. A protocol fact named after one of its readers
// is the shape that becomes two constants the day a third arrives.
export let BuiltinProtocolSoleMethodIndex = 0 as WitnessMethodIndex

// ⭐⭐ **`a.equals(b)` THROUGH THE `Equatable` WITNESS — `witnessCall(eqWitness, method 0, a, b) -> bool`
// (0/1), and the ONE shape every runtime that compares two values of a conformer type emits.** For an `int`
// element/key the slot holds `int.equals`, so this is `a == b`; for a `String` it is `__str_eq`'s content
// compare; for a user type it is that type's own `equals`.
//
// ⚠ The result type is `builtinProtocolResultStdType`'s and NOT a hand-written `i64`, because a runtime
// emitting this is a THIRD party to the slot's signature (beside the dispatch site and the impl) and a
// slot's signature is checked exactly on wasm — see that function.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`operandsAreFloat` IS THE FLOAT CONTAINER SLOT, AND IT IS A REGISTER-FILE FACT RATHER THAN A
// WIDTH ONE.** A float element is stored in an `Array`'s slot as its 64 BITS
// (`Parser.containerElementWord`: `bitcastF64ToI64` in, `bitcastI64ToF64` out), so a runtime that reads two
// slots holds two GPR words — while `float.equals(self, other)` declares f64 formals and reads XMM. Handed
// over unconverted the callee would compare whatever was left in xmm0/xmm1 (a silent wrong answer on x64)
// and wasm would trap `indirect call type mismatch`; moved without the REINTERPRETATION the x64 emitter
// refuses outright (*"a register-to-register move from rax to xmm0 crosses register files"*). So the two
// operands are bitcast back and the mask is DERIVED from the very formals that decided the bitcast —
// `floatParamMaskOf`, never a written-out `3` — so the two halves of one fact cannot disagree.
//
// ⚠ The retired `CharacterSet` member probe passed `false` and that was not an omission: its key was `Character` by
// construction, and a float never reaches a hash table's probe at all — a float type ARGUMENT is E2062 one
// gate earlier, because a type parameter is an opaque general-purpose slot a float has no way through.
export function emitEquatableWitnessEquals(module StdModule, block IrBlock, eqWitness ValueId, a ValueId, b ValueId, operandsAreFloat bool, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let formals = twoF64Params() if operandsAreFloat else twoI64Params()
	var lhs = a
	var rhs = b
	if operandsAreFloat 'reinterpretSlotBits'
		lhs = emitUnary(module, block: block, operand: a, opcode: StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastI64ToF64, operandType: StdType.i64, ids: ids)
		rhs = emitUnary(module, block: block, operand: b, opcode: StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastI64ToF64, operandType: StdType.i64, ids: ids)
	end 'reinterpretSlotBits'

	let equal = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.witnessCall(equal, witnessAddr: eqWitness, methodIndex: BuiltinProtocolSoleMethodIndex, resultType: StdReturnType.value(builtinProtocolResultStdType(BoolConformerName)), argFloatMask: floatParamMaskOf(formals), args: twoArgs(lhs, second: rhs)))
	return equal
end 'emitEquatableWitnessEquals'

// Install the synthesized builtin-type conformance impls when — and only when — a witness table references
// them (P1.7a slice 2b + P1.7b). Each impl NAME is `qualifiedMethodName(<conformer>, <method>)`, the SAME
// construction `ensureWitnessTable` names its reloc target by, so the built function, the reloc, and the
// dispatch cannot disagree on the symbol.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`arrayConformerName` IS THE ONE SYMBOL HALF THIS FILE CANNOT SPELL (ARR6).** Five of the six
// conformers are compiler-owned spellings — three keywords and two names no declaration may take — so their
// impl symbols are joined from constants here. The SIXTH is the corpus's array record, whose name the
// program's own `BuiltinArrayLiteral` declaration chooses (`ProgramSignatures.arrayRecordDeclaredName`), and
// it was joined from the constant `b"Array"` while the witness slots that REFERENCE those symbols are
// stamped with `conformerNameOfType`'s discovered answer. The two agreed only because `stdlib/Array.maxon`
// declares the record as `Array`; under any other spelling the walk below would look for a symbol the module
// never names and the referenced one would go unbuilt — a link failure, or worse a silently absent
// conformance. Threaded in, the reference and the definition are one join of one name.
export function installBuiltinConformanceRuntime(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, ordering EnumLayout, arrayConformerName ByteArray)
	// The candidate list the reference walk matches against, built from the very `ByteArray`s the builders
	// are installed under — one join per symbol, so the name the walk looks for and the name the function
	// carries cannot be two joins that happen to agree.
	//
	// ⚠ **MINTING AND REGISTERING ARE ONE ACT (`candidateImplName`), because as two they were one list
	// written twice.** A symbol bound here but missed out of the candidate array is never REFERENCED as far
	// as the walk can tell, so its `if` never fires, so the impl is never built — and the program that needs
	// it dies at link time naming a symbol whose builder is sitting right here. There is now no way to bind
	// one without registering it.
	//
	// `bool` has neither `Hashable` nor `Equatable`, so there is no `bool.hash`/`bool.equals` to mint —
	// and `String` has no `Comparable`, so no `String.compare`. Those three holes are one fact, stated in
	// `isIntrinsicBuiltinConformance` and read from there rather than restated here. ⚠ `Character` looks
	// like a fourth and is not: it conforms to all three (A5m-c), and the arm that said otherwise was a
	// claim about `stdlib/Character.maxon` that the file contradicts.
	var impls = ByteArrayArray.create()
	let intHash = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: IntConformerName, methodName: HashMethodName)
	let intEquals = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: IntConformerName, methodName: EqualsMethodName)
	let intCompare = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: IntConformerName, methodName: CompareMethodName)
	let floatHash = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: FloatConformerName, methodName: HashMethodName)
	let floatEquals = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: FloatConformerName, methodName: EqualsMethodName)
	let floatCompare = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: FloatConformerName, methodName: CompareMethodName)
	let boolCompare = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: BoolConformerName, methodName: CompareMethodName)
	let strHash = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: StringConformerName, methodName: HashMethodName)
	let strEquals = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: StringConformerName, methodName: EqualsMethodName)
	let charHash = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: CharacterConformerName, methodName: HashMethodName)
	let charEquals = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: CharacterConformerName, methodName: EqualsMethodName)
	let charCompare = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: CharacterConformerName, methodName: CompareMethodName)
	let arrayHash = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: arrayConformerName, methodName: HashMethodName)
	let arrayEquals = candidateImplName(impls, conformerName: arrayConformerName, methodName: EqualsMethodName)

	// ⭐ ASKED ONCE, BEFORE THE FIRST INSTALL — which is what makes the answers independent of the order
	// they are acted on in. A scan run BETWEEN installs would see the ops of the impls this very function
	// had already appended; none of the bodies calls another today, but that is a property of their current
	// shape rather than of the mechanism, and the single up-front call needs no such promise.
	let referenced = referencedBuiltinImpls(module, globalData: globalData, impls: impls)

	if referenced.contains(intHash) 'intHash'
		buildIntHash(module, name: intHash)
	end 'intHash'

	if referenced.contains(intEquals) 'intEquals'
		buildBuiltinEquals(module, name: intEquals, operandType: StdType.i64)
	end 'intEquals'

	if referenced.contains(intCompare) 'intCompare'
		buildOrderingCompare(module, name: intCompare, operandType: StdType.i64, ordering: ordering)
	end 'intCompare'

	if referenced.contains(floatHash) 'floatHash'
		buildFloatHash(module, name: floatHash)
	end 'floatHash'

	if referenced.contains(floatEquals) 'floatEquals'
		buildBuiltinEquals(module, name: floatEquals, operandType: StdType.f64)
	end 'floatEquals'

	if referenced.contains(floatCompare) 'floatCompare'
		buildFloatCompare(module, name: floatCompare, ordering: ordering)
	end 'floatCompare'

	// ⭐ `bool.compare` IS `int.compare`'s graph, and that is a fact about the STD TIER rather than a
	// shortcut. Both reference compilers write bool's body as `if self then a = 1` twice over an int pair
	// (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:73-90`) only because the SOURCE language has no bool→int cast; a
	// Std `i1` already IS 0 or 1 in its machine word, so the mapping is the identity and the cascade is
	// literally the same ops. One builder, two symbols — the symbol must still be `bool.compare`, because
	// that is what the parser mints and what a `__witness_bool.Comparable` slot would name.
	if referenced.contains(boolCompare) 'boolCompare'
		buildOrderingCompare(module, name: boolCompare, operandType: StdType.i64, ordering: ordering)
	end 'boolCompare'

	if referenced.contains(strHash) 'strHash'
		buildStrHash(module, name: strHash)
	end 'strHash'

	// `String.equals` is `__str_eq`'s byte-compare emitted under the witness-visible symbol — ONE graph, two
	// names (`StringRuntime.buildStrEq`), so the operator path and the witness path cannot drift.
	if referenced.contains(strEquals) 'strEquals'
		buildStrEq(module, name: strEquals)
	end 'strEquals'

	// ⭐ **`Character.hash` IS `String.hash`'s GRAPH AND `Character.equals` IS `String.equals`'s, UNDER
	// THEIR OWN SYMBOLS** (P1.8 Slice D) — the `bool.compare`/`int.compare` arrangement one type over. Both
	// types ARE the fused byte record (`TypeRules.tagIsByteRecord`), so djb2 over `buffer@0`/`length@8` and
	// `__str_eq`'s content compare answer for a Character exactly as they answer for a String; two
	// transcriptions could only ever come to disagree about what equality MEANS for a pair that hashes alike.
	// The SYMBOLS must still be Character's — see `CharacterConformerName` for why they may not be shared.
	if referenced.contains(charHash) 'charHash'
		buildStrHash(module, name: charHash)
	end 'charHash'

	if referenced.contains(charEquals) 'charEquals'
		buildStrEq(module, name: charEquals)
	end 'charEquals'

	// ⭐ **`Character.compare` IS THE ONE IMPL A `String` HAS NO TWIN FOR** (A5m-c). The graph is the
	// byte-record lexicographic cascade, installed here under the WITNESS-visible symbol and under
	// `__char_cmp` by `installGraphemeRuntime` for the operator door — `buildStrEq`'s two-symbol
	// arrangement, one builder.
	if referenced.contains(charCompare) 'charCompare'
		buildByteRecordCompare(module, name: charCompare, ordering: ordering)
	end 'charCompare'

	// ⭐⭐ **`Array` IS THE SIXTH CONFORMER, AND ITS TWO BODIES ARE `ManagedMemoryRuntime`'s (array-hashable)** — that
	// file owns the 48-byte record and the packed-aware byte extent, exactly as `StringRuntime` owns
	// `buildStrEq`'s. It joins by the same rule as every impl here: one symbol per (conformer, method), built
	// iff the emitted module names it.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND IT IS THE `__str_eq` / `String.equals` ARRANGEMENT, NOT A THIRD SHAPE.** The very same graphs
	// are ALSO installed under `__managed_hash`/`__managed_eq` by `installManagedMemoryRuntime`, which is what the DIRECT
	// `arr.hash()` / `a == b` dispatch calls; the witness-visible pair is these. Two symbols, one builder
	// each — see `ManagedMemoryRuntime.ManagedHashName` for why the direct door may not name `Array.<method>` the way the
	// `String` and `int` doors name theirs.
	if referenced.contains(arrayHash) 'arrayHash'
		buildArrayHash(module, name: arrayHash)
	end 'arrayHash'

	if referenced.contains(arrayEquals) 'arrayEquals'
		buildArrayEquals(module, name: arrayEquals)
	end 'arrayEquals'
end 'installBuiltinConformanceRuntime'

// Mint one impl's symbol and enrol it as a candidate in ONE act — see the installer for the drift this
// closes. It is `qualifiedMethodName` plus the enrolment, not a wrapper around either: the returned
// `ByteArray` is the SAME one the array now holds, which is what lets `referenced.contains(<name>)` compare
// the identical bytes the walk searched for.
function candidateImplName(impls ByteArrayArray, conformerName ByteArray, methodName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	let name = qualifiedMethodName(conformerName, methodName: methodName)
	impls.push(name)
	return name
end 'candidateImplName'

// ⭐ **WHICH OF `impls` THIS PROGRAM NAMES BUT DOES NOT DEFINE** — the install's ONE question, answered for
// every candidate at once so the module is walked ONCE rather than once per impl. Both reference kinds are
// collected here (see the header): every pending `.rdata` funcAbs64 slot target, and every function name the
// emitted ops spell.
//
// ⚠ **"NAMES BUT DOES NOT DEFINE" — AND THE SECOND HALF IS NOT PEDANTRY, IT IS A MEASURED WRONG ANSWER.**
// A synthesized impl exists to SUPPLY a symbol nothing else provides; a symbol the module already defines
// needs none, and installing one anyway appends a SECOND function under that name, which the by-name link
// then resolves to whichever it likes. **This paragraph used to except `int.*` — *"`int` is a keyword, so no
// user method can wear that qualifier"* — and W133 ended that**: an `extension int implements Hashable`
// declares a real `int.hash`, so all three primitives now collide exactly as `String.*` always could. The
// narrowing below was already the right shape and needed no change; what needed changing is the reason
// written beside it, because a premise that has quietly expired is how the next reader talks themselves out
// of the guard. `String.*` was the first: a user `type String` with its own `hash()` mints exactly the
// symbol `String.hash`. MEASURED — crediting that call site made `String.create(7).hash()` return **5381**,
// djb2's seed read off the user's box, instead of `7`. So the candidates are narrowed to the UNDEFINED ones
// before a single reference is credited.
//
// ⚠ It walks the FLAT `module.ops` array, ORPHANS INCLUDED, and that is deliberate rather than a slip of
// the `liveOpIndices` rule. The rule exists for passes asking *"what is actually here?"*, where a dead op is
// a false positive that matters; this asks *"could the program reference this?"*, where the only cost of a
// false positive is an impl `eliminateDeadFunctions` then prunes — while `liveOpIndices` would allocate an
// ops-sized index array PER FUNCTION to answer it. Over-approximating is free here and under-approximating
// is a link failure, so the walk is deliberately the cheap, wide one.
function referencedBuiltinImpls(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, impls ByteArrayArray) returns FunctionNameSet
	var referenced = FunctionNameSet.create()

	let synthesizable = undefinedImplNames(module, impls: impls)
	if synthesizable.count() == 0 'nothingToSynthesize'
		return referenced
	end 'nothingToSynthesize'

	for reloc in globalData.pendingRdataRelocs 'eachRdataSlot'
		noteBuiltinImplReference(reloc.funcName, impls: synthesizable, referenced: referenced)
	end 'eachRdataSlot'

	for opIndex in 0 upto module.ops.count() 'eachOp'
		noteOpBuiltinImplReference(try module.ops.get(opIndex) otherwise panic("referencedBuiltinImpls: op index {opIndex} below count {module.ops.count()} is not in module.ops"), impls: synthesizable, referenced: referenced)
	end 'eachOp'

	return referenced
end 'referencedBuiltinImpls'

// The candidate impls no function in the module already defines — see `referencedBuiltinImpls`' second
// warning for the wrong answer this exists to prevent.
//
// ⭐ **ONE WALK OF `module.functions`, NOT ONE PER CANDIDATE (A2l/2).** It used to ask a
// `moduleDefinesFunction` helper per impl, and each of those rescanned the WHOLE array — O(impls ×
// functions), which with a constant eleven candidates meant ELEVEN full walks of every compile's function
// array. (The comment here claimed the array was "walked once"; it was not. That is the shape this batch
// exists to remove, in its cost form rather than its correctness form.)
//
// The eleven questions are the same question asked of one array, so it is asked once per FUNCTION instead:
// the outer walk reads each `IrFunction` record exactly once — the expensive half — and the inner test is
// the same eleven byte compares that were being made anyway, almost all of which fail on length, and which
// stop at the first hit because a name can answer for at most one candidate.
//
// ⚠ **THE LADDER CANNOT SEE THIS ONE, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE INSTRUMENT.** `installBuiltinConformanceRuntime`
// runs between the pipeline and `buildBackend`, outside every `PhaseProbe` bracket, so its cost lands in
// `unattributed` and no per-phase row moves. The claim here is structural (eleven walks became one), not a
// measured delta — do not read a flat scale-test row as evidence about it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT KEYED THROUGH `FunctionNameIndex`, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** No checked index exists at
// this point in the compile — the installers are still appending, and this one appends too — so building
// one here would add a module-sized map to EVERY compile to answer eleven questions. It would also buy no
// guarantee: this is a MEMBERSHIP test, not a resolution, so it has no tie to break. Two functions of one
// name would make it answer `defined` either way, which is the same answer, and the pair itself is refused
// downstream where an index does exist (`DeadFunctionElimination`).
function undefinedImplNames(module StdModule, impls ByteArrayArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	// At most eleven entries, and it is a SET rather than a per-candidate flag column so both loops can walk
	// their arrays by BORROW — neither the function records nor the candidate names are retained to answer a
	// byte compare.
	var definedCandidates = FunctionNameSet.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for impl in impls 'eachImpl'
			if func.name.equals(impl) 'definesCandidate'
				definedCandidates.insert(impl)
				break
			end 'definesCandidate'
		end 'eachImpl'
	end 'eachFunc'

	var undefined = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for impl in impls 'eachCandidate'
		if not definedCandidates.contains(impl) 'notDefined'
			undefined.push(impl)
		end 'notDefined'
	end 'eachCandidate'
	return undefined
end 'undefinedImplNames'

// The function NAME one `StdOp` spells, credited if it is one of the candidate impls. THREE variants carry
// one — `call`, `tryCall` and `funcAddr` — the same three `DeadFunctionElimination.markCalleeOf` walks for
// its reachability edges, and for the same reason: those are the ops that can make a symbol required.
//
// ⚠ **YES, THAT MATCH IS WRITTEN TWICE OVER THE SAME UNION, AND THE FOLD IS REFUSED FOR A COST REASON, NOT
// A TIDINESS ONE.** The shared half is *"which variant carries a callee name, and in which field"*; the
// halves that differ are what each does with it (mark-and-queue there, credit-into-a-set here). Sharing it
// needs a per-op extractor answering "a name, or nothing", and this language has no free spelling of that:
// a payload union BOXES (16 bytes per op — the exact cost the optimizer just deleted from this rung's
// receiver classifier, see `BuiltinConformer`'s header), an out-`ByteArrayArray` pushes a MANAGED ByteArray
// per hit, a closure parameter captures, and an empty-name sentinel is a wrong answer waiting for a callee
// the module really does not name. `markCalleeOf` walks every LIVE op of every function; a box per op there
// is not a refactor, it is a regression.
//
// What binds the copies instead is a COMPILE-TIME CHECK, not this comment. The arms RANGE over the whole
// union in declaration order, ending at the current last variant, so a variant appended past it makes BOTH
// matches non-exhaustive — **RE-MEASURED 2026-08-11 by appending a variant and building: E2026 "match on
// enum 'StdOp' is not exhaustive, missing: probeAppendedVariant"**, at this function's own `match` and at
// 30-odd others, which names the variant. (The quoted name was `'Op'` until that measurement — a type
// nothing declares. ⚠ And the noun is *"enum"* here for a reason that is easy to mis-correct: `StdOp` is
// `export union StdOp`, but this sentence is the BOOTSTRAP's, because shv2's own source is compiled by
// `maxon build maxon-shv2`, and the bootstrap HARDCODES `"enum"` at that one site while branching on
// `IsUnion` at its E3034 sites four lines away. shv2 itself derives the word from `EnumLayout.kindWord()`
// and would say "union" — pinned by `enum-match-exhaustive/error.union-not-exhaustive`. So this transcript
// changes on the day shv2 compiles itself, and not before.) A new variant carrying a callee name must be added as its own arm in both, and neither
// can be forgotten silently. (The residual is the human who *extends the last band* instead of adding an
// arm; that mistake is equally available in `markCalleeOf` and is what these per-band comments exist to
// make loud in review.)
function noteOpBuiltinImplReference(op StdOp, impls ByteArrayArray, referenced FunctionNameSet)
	match op 'op'
		// arith + control + the call band up to `call`: pure computation, branches, `ret`, `param`.
		const to param then break 'op'
		call(_, callee, _) then noteBuiltinImplReference(callee, impls: impls, referenced: referenced)
		// `retVoid` … `rdataAddr`: returns, `.data`/`.rdata` ADDRESSES (labels, not functions), loads,
		// stores, and the two OS primitives that name no Maxon function.
		retVoid to rdataAddr then break 'op'
		tryCall(_, _, callee, _) then noteBuiltinImplReference(callee, impls: impls, referenced: referenced)
		// `errorReturn` reads two local virtuals; `osExit` is an OS primitive.
		errorReturn to osExit then break 'op'
		funcAddr(_, funcName) then noteBuiltinImplReference(funcName, impls: impls, referenced: referenced)
		// `callIndirect`/`witnessCall` consume a code pointer some `funcAddr` or `.rdata` slot already
		// produced, so they name no symbol; nor do the appended `os*` primitives or `switch`.
		callIndirect to osWriteFile then break 'op'
		// The wall-clock read names no Maxon function either — its callee is a Win32 import slot.
		osReadWallClock then break 'op'
		// The THROWING witness call consumes a `.rdata` code pointer and names no symbol, exactly as
		// `witnessCall` does not.
		witnessTryCall then break 'op'
		// Every file op takes buffers and handles and names no Maxon function (R4.1 + R4.2).
		osFileOpenRead to osFileAttributes then break 'op'
		// A3n: the RENDERED panic reads a message pointer; in every other respect it is `osPanic`.
		osPanicValue then break 'op'
		osCommandLine then break 'op'
		// W5. Both take an immediate selector or a caller's buffer and name no Maxon function.
		osStdinHandle then break 'op'
		osExecutablePath then break 'op'
		// W35. A stream write takes a buffer and a length and names no Maxon function — `osWriteStdout`'s
		// own case, one stream over.
		osWriteStderr then break 'op'
		// A frame address names no Maxon function.
		stackRecordAddr then break 'op'
		// O3: the three DebugStream host primitives call Win32 IMPORTS, never a Maxon function — the file
		// band's reason one line up.
		osOpenNamedSegment to osTickCountMs then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteOpBuiltinImplReference'

// Credit `name` if it is one of the candidate impls. A linear scan, which is why the candidates are an array
// rather than a set: at a handful of entries the compare is cheaper than a hash, and this runs per op.
function noteBuiltinImplReference(name ByteArray, impls ByteArrayArray, referenced FunctionNameSet)
	for impl in impls 'eachImpl'
		if name.equals(impl) 'match'
			referenced.insert(impl)
			return
		end 'match'
	end 'eachImpl'
end 'noteBuiltinImplReference'

// `int.hash(self) -> HashValue` — `self & 0xFFFFFFFF`, one `and` against the low-32 mask. The receiver is a
// by-value scalar i64 in arg-0 (the witness dispatch passes `self.item`, a scalar `T` field read, straight in
// — no pointer indirection), so the whole body is a mask and a return.
function buildIntHash(module StdModule, name ByteArray)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(builtinProtocolResultStdType(HashValueTypeName)), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId

	returnLow32Masked(module, block: entry, value: receiver, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildIntHash'

// ⭐⭐ `float.hash(self) -> HashValue` (P1.7a's LAST slice) — the low 32 bits of `self`'s IEEE-754
// pattern: ONE reinterpret, then the same masked return `int.hash` and `String.hash` end on.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT OMITS THE `-0.0` BRANCH BOTH REFERENCES WRITE, AND THAT IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE.**
// `stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:31-36` opens with `if bits == i64.min return 0`, and under a
// LOW-32 mask that branch cannot be reached distinguishably: `-0.0` is `0x8000000000000000`, whose low
// half is already zero, so the fallthrough returns 0 for it — the same 0 the branch would. Emitting it
// would cost a 64-bit const, a compare, a conditional branch and two extra blocks on every
// `float.hash` call to compute what the `and` already computes. The property the branch was buying —
// the two zeros hash alike — is instead held by `assertSignBitAboveHashMask`, which fires if
// `HashValue` ever widens past 32 bits and the branch becomes load-bearing.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE ONLY BUILTIN IMPL THAT ASKS A QUESTION ABOUT BITS RATHER THAN ABOUT A NUMBER**, and
// that is why it needed a new Std opcode. `int.hash` masks its receiver directly because an `int`
// ALREADY is its bits; a float's bits live in an XMM register, so reaching them means a genuine
// cross-register-file transfer — `StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastF64ToI64`, one instruction on all three
// backends (`movq r64, xmm` / `FMOV Xd, Dn` / `i64.reinterpret_f64`), no memory round trip and no
// `alloca`. Everything after that transfer is `int.hash`'s arithmetic over an ordinary i64.
//
// ⚠ **THE PARAMETER IS DECLARED f64, WHICH IS WHY THIS USES `emitTypedParams` AND NOT `emitParams`.**
// The all-i64 `emitParams` states the GPR-only internal ABI every other one-parameter runtime chunk
// uses; here the receiver arrives in an XMM register, and the declared type is what both ends read
// (`calleeParamClass` to route the argument, `lowerParam` to capture it). The RESULT is still the
// uniform `builtinProtocolResultStdType(HashValue)` = i64 — one spelling, as every witness-visible
// impl's must be.
//
// ⚠⚠ **NOTHING IS NORMALIZED AT ALL — NOT NaN, AND NOT EVEN THE SIGN.** Every NaN hashes to whatever
// its own payload masks to, so two NaNs built differently can hash differently, and `-3.14` hashes
// exactly as `3.14` does. Both are correct, and the SAME argument covers them: the contract `Hashable`
// imposes is *equal values hash equal*, never *unequal values hash differently*. `float.equals` is
// plain IEEE, so `NaN.equals(NaN)` is FALSE and NaN owes nothing to anybody; and the only
// `equals`-equal pair whose bits differ at all is `±0.0`, which the mask already settles (above).
// Both references leave NaN raw for the same reason; `primitive-hashable/float.hash.nan` and
// `float.hash.negative` pin both behaviours so a later reader does not "fix" them.
//
// ⚠ `float.compare`'s NaN preamble is NOT the counter-example it looks like: `compare` implements a
// TOTAL order, which IEEE does not give, so it must place NaN by hand. `hash` needs no order at all.
function buildFloatHash(module StdModule, name ByteArray)
	assertSignBitAboveHashMask()

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(builtinProtocolResultStdType(HashValueTypeName)), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneF64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitTypedParams(module, block: entry, paramTypes: func.paramTypes)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId

	// Reinterpret, then hand the i64 to the SAME masked return `int.hash` and `String.hash` end on.
	let bits = emitUnary(module, block: entry, operand: receiver, opcode: StdUnaryOpcode.bitcastF64ToI64, operandType: StdType.f64, ids: ids)
	returnLow32Masked(module, block: entry, value: bits, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildFloatHash'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PRECONDITION THAT LETS `buildFloatHash` OMIT THE `-0.0` BRANCH BOTH REFERENCES WRITE** —
// stated as a CHECK rather than as a comment, because it is a dependence between two constants and a
// comment is a test nobody wrote.
//
// `-0.0` and `+0.0` compare EQUAL under IEEE, so `Hashable` requires one hash for them, and their bit
// patterns differ in exactly one place: bit 63. A mask that discards bit 63 therefore normalizes the
// pair for free, and `HashLow32Mask` keeps only bits 0–31, so it does. **Widen the mask to reach bit 63
// — which is what widening `HashValue` past 32 bits would do — and that stops being true, silently.**
// `float.hash` would keep compiling and keep passing every test that hashes a nonzero float, while
// quietly answering `0x8000000000000000` for `-0.0` against `0` for `+0.0`: an `equals`-equal pair with
// two hashes, which is a wrong answer inside any hash container that ever holds a negative zero.
//
// ⚠ It is asked of the CONSTANTS, not of a hashed value, so it cannot be satisfied by luck — no input
// reaches it. MEASURED RED: with `HashLow32Mask` set to `-1` it panics on the first program that
// installs the impl, which is the only evidence that a check of this shape is live rather than decorative.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT CHECKS THE MASK'S WIDTH, NOT THE FOLD'S OPERATION, AND THAT GAP CANNOT BE CLOSED HERE.** It
// asks only "does `and`-ing with `HashLow32Mask` discard bit 63?". Change `returnLow32Masked` to FOLD
// rather than truncate — an xor-down, a rotate, the very "rotate / widened `HashValue`" its own comment
// floats — and bit 63 reaches the low half again: `-0.0` and `+0.0` would hash differently while this
// assert passed VACUOUSLY, because the mask it inspects would still be 32 bits wide. Nothing available
// at compile time can see that (it is a property of a graph this function does not read), so it is
// stated out loud instead: **anyone changing that fold must re-derive whether ±0.0 still agree, and
// restore the explicit `-0.0` branch if they do not.**
//
// So the moment that widening happens, this fires and names the remedy. It costs one compile-time test
// per program that installs the impl.
function assertSignBitAboveHashMask()
	if (NegativeZeroBits and HashLow32Mask) != 0 'signBitSurvivesMask'
		panic("assertSignBitAboveHashMask: `HashLow32Mask` no longer discards IEEE-754's sign bit, so `-0.0` and `+0.0` no longer hash alike — `buildFloatHash` must regain the explicit `-0.0 -> 0` branch both reference compilers carry (stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:31-36)")
	end 'signBitSurvivesMask'
end 'assertSignBitAboveHashMask'

// ⭐ **`return self & 0xFFFFFFFF` — the tail EVERY builtin hasher ends on, written once.** All three
// (`int`, `float`, `String`) fold their accumulated i64 down to `HashValue`'s width and return it, and
// the three had begun to spell that three times: the CONSTANT was already shared, but the SHAPE was
// not, so a hash that ever has to leave differently — a rotate, a widened `HashValue`, a range
// assertion on the way out — would be changed at one site and read as complete there.
//
// ⚠ **CHANGING THIS FROM A TRUNCATION TO A FOLD IS NOT A LOCAL EDIT.** `buildFloatHash` omits the
// `-0.0` branch both reference compilers write, and the ONLY reason that is safe is that this `and`
// DISCARDS bit 63. An xor-down or a rotate would carry the sign bit into the low half and make ±0.0
// hash differently, silently — and `assertSignBitAboveHashMask` would NOT catch it (it inspects the
// mask's width, not this operation). Read that function before touching this line.
//
// `block` is the block the value is live in and becomes the returning block, which is why the caller
// passes it rather than this minting one: `int.hash`/`float.hash` end in their entry, `String.hash` in
// its loop exit.
function returnLow32Masked(module StdModule, block IrBlock, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: HashLow32Mask, ids: ids)
	let masked = emitBin(module, block: block, a: value, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.ret(masked))
end 'returnLow32Masked'

// The two-operand Std function every builtin `equals`/`compare` impl is: two parameters of its OWN operand
// type, returning the type the INTERFACE METHOD it implements declares. ONE place where the declared ABI and
// the operand width meet, so a caller cannot pair an f64 compare with i64 parameters — which would route the
// arguments through the WRONG register file at every call site (`calleeParamClass` reads exactly these
// declared types) with nothing to report it.
//
// ⭐ **THE RESULT TYPE IS THE INTERFACE'S, NOT A UNIFORM MACHINE WORD, AND THAT IS A WITNESS-TABLE
// OBLIGATION (P1.7a slice 2b-iv-B).** These impls are installed into a witness table typed by the interface,
// and there is no `ConformanceCheck` to hold them to it — they are synthesized, so nothing but this line
// makes their declared signature agree with the one every DISPATCH SITE derives from
// `Equatable`/`Comparable` itself. `equals` returns `bool` (an `i1`) and `compare` an `Ordering` tag (an
// `i64`); declaring both `i64` was invisible on the register targets, where a `bool` rides a 64-bit register
// as readily as a tag, and on wasm it was a **`wasm trap: indirect call type mismatch`** — the dispatch site
// declared `(i64,i64)->i32` against an impl typed `(i64,i64)->i64`. MEASURED: 7 `Equatable`-via-constraint
// spec cases across `equatable`/`primitive-conformance`/`string-conformance` trapped under wasmtime while
// answering correctly on x64. The PARAMETERS stay i64 — the uniform indirect ABI passes every non-float
// argument as a machine word, so those already agree.
function createTwoOperandImpl(name ByteArray, operandType StdType, resultType StdType) returns IrFunction
	return IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(resultType), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoOperandParams(operandType), returnLine: 0)
end 'createTwoOperandImpl'

// The parameter list for `createTwoOperandImpl`, routed through `MmRuntime`'s shared param shapes rather
// than minting a third array builder. An integer operand of ANY width declares i64 parameters: every
// synthesized chunk uses the GPR-only internal ABI, in which a machine word carries a `bool` as readily as
// an `int` (see `MmRuntime.emitParams`).
//
// ⚠ **CALLED ONCE PER IMPL — the `param` ops read the array off the FUNCTION** (`func.paramTypes`, which
// `createFromStd` stores by reference), never from a second call to this. Two calls would be two
// derivations of one fact, free to disagree the day an operand type maps to a param shape conditionally,
// and they cost one `StdTypeArray` each: MEASURED at +2 allocations per installed impl.
function twoOperandParams(operandType StdType) returns StdTypeArray
	match operandType 'shape'
		i1 to u64 then return twoI64Params()
		f32 then panic("twoOperandParams: shv2's `float` is f64 throughout — nothing produces an f32, so no builtin conformance impl declares one")
		f64 then return twoF64Params()
	end 'shape'
end 'twoOperandParams'

// `<conformer>.equals(self, other) -> bool` — `self == other`, materialized as 1/0 off a fused `cmp`+`jcc`
// (there is no `setcc` at this tier). The `__str_eq` verdict shape: a compare terminates the entry into a
// true block and a false block, each returning its constant. Both operands are by-value scalars in
// arg-0/arg-1.
//
// ⭐ ONE BODY FOR `int.equals` AND `float.equals`, differing only in the operand width — which is exactly
// what `StdOp.cmp`'s `operandType` is for, and what makes the f64 form a `ucomisd` rather than a `cmp`.
//
// ⚠ **`float.equals` IS IEEE `==`, SO `NaN.equals(NaN)` IS FALSE — and that is deliberately NOT what
// `float.compare` says about the same pair.** `compare` implements a TOTAL order (NaN at the bottom, two
// NaNs `equalTo`); `equals` implements IEEE equality. Both reference compilers pin the split
// (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:39-41` is a bare `self == other` beside a NaN-aware `compare`) and the
// canonical corpus pins both halves, so the asymmetry is the specification rather than an oversight here.
function buildBuiltinEquals(module StdModule, name ByteArray, operandType StdType)
	var func = createTwoOperandImpl(name, operandType: operandType, resultType: builtinProtocolResultStdType(BoolConformerName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var equalBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"equal")
	var notEqualBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notEqual")

	emitTypedParams(module, block: entry, paramTypes: func.paramTypes)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId
	let other = 1 as ValueId

	let isEqual = emitCmp(module, block: entry, a: receiver, b: other, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids, operandType: operandType)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isEqual, thenBlockId: equalBlock.id, elseBlockId: notEqualBlock.id))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: equalBlock, value: PredicateTrue, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: notEqualBlock, value: PredicateFalse, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildBuiltinEquals'

// One `a <pred> b` test terminating `block` into two successors — the fused `cmp`+`jcc` every compare impl
// is built out of. Its result is read ONLY by the branch, so the backend materializes no register for it.
function emitOrderingTest(module StdModule, block IrBlock, a ValueId, b ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, operandType StdType, thenBlock IrBlock, elseBlock IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let verdict = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: a, b: b, pred: pred, ids: ids, operandType: operandType)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(verdict, thenBlockId: thenBlock.id, elseBlockId: elseBlock.id))
end 'emitOrderingTest'

// ⭐ A block that RETURNS one `Ordering` case's tag — the exit every compare impl's verdict paths land on.
//
// ⭐ The tag comes from `EnumLayout.tagOfCase` — read OFF the `Ordering` layout, so reordering
// `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s own declaration moves these bodies and the `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` lowering's tag
// tests together. The bootstrap writes them as literals at its consumer sites with nothing tying them back to
// the declaration; that is a latent coupling shv2 deliberately does not copy. Naming the CASE here rather than
// passing a number is what keeps that derivation at one call per verdict, across both compare impls.
//
// ⚠ **THE LAYOUT IS HANDED IN RATHER THAN FETCHED (W3), AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES "the same declaration" TRUE
// RATHER THAN INTENDED.** It is `stdlib/Interfaces.maxon`'s, read ONCE per compile by the caller of
// `installBuiltinConformanceRuntime` / `installGraphemeRuntime` off `Project.enumTypes`; these builders sit
// at the Std tier with no `Project` in reach, and a second door to the layout would be a second thing to keep
// pointed at the same registry.
function emitOrderingVerdict(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ordering EnumLayout, caseName ByteArray, ids ValueMinter)
	let tag = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ordering.tagOfCase(caseName), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.ret(tag))
end 'emitOrderingVerdict'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ORDERED THREE-WAY CASCADE, WRITTEN ONCE** — `a < b` ⇒ `lessThan`, else `a > b` ⇒ `greaterThan`,
// else fall through to `equalTo`, entered at `head`. Both reference compilers state exactly this cascade
// (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:11-19`), and its `<`/`>` are plain scalar compares, so there is no
// recursion back into `Comparable`. Two fused `cmp`+`jcc` tests, each falling through to the next, and one
// `const`-returning block per case.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS SHARED BECAUSE THE ALTERNATIVE FAILS AS A WRONG ANSWER, NOT AS A BUILD ERROR.**
// `int.compare`/`bool.compare` are nothing BUT this cascade (entered at `entry`), and `float.compare` is this
// cascade behind a NaN preamble (entered at `ordered`). Written out at both sites — as it briefly was, held
// together by a comment saying *"the same cascade"* — a later edit to either could reach one impl and miss
// the other, and the two would then disagree about what `lessThan` MEANS for programs that use both. Nothing
// would fail to compile and no golden would move; `int` and `float` would simply order differently. One
// callee is what makes that impossible.
//
// The four verdict/continuation blocks are the CALLER's, not this function's, because their creation ORDER is
// the order they are emitted in — and `float.compare` must interleave its preamble's blocks ahead of them.
//
// ⚠ **`buildByteRecordCompare`'s BYTE LOOP DELIBERATELY DOES NOT CALL THIS, AND CALLS `emitOrderingTest`
// TWICE INSTEAD.** A tied BYTE must continue the walk rather than answer `equalTo`, so the loop needs the two
// TESTS without the three verdicts — and extracting a tests-only helper for both to share would move the
// `lessThan` const from between the two tests to after them, renumbering every value in `int.compare`,
// `bool.compare` and `float.compare`. That is the same reordering `emitDjb2HashOverByteRange` measured at 42
// moved fragments, bought here for two lines. The shared unit is `emitOrderingTest`, and it already is.
function emitOrderedCascade(module StdModule, head IrBlock, a ValueId, b ValueId, operandType StdType, ordering EnumLayout, lessBlock IrBlock, notLessBlock IrBlock, greaterBlock IrBlock, equalBlock IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: head, a: a, b: b, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, operandType: operandType, thenBlock: lessBlock, elseBlock: notLessBlock, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingVerdict(module, block: lessBlock, ordering: ordering, caseName: OrderingLessThanCaseName, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: notLessBlock, a: a, b: b, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, operandType: operandType, thenBlock: greaterBlock, elseBlock: equalBlock, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingVerdict(module, block: greaterBlock, ordering: ordering, caseName: OrderingGreaterThanCaseName, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingVerdict(module, block: equalBlock, ordering: ordering, caseName: OrderingEqualToCaseName, ids: ids)
end 'emitOrderedCascade'

// `<conformer>.compare(self, other) -> Ordering` for a scalar whose MACHINE ORDER IS ITS TOTAL ORDER (P1.7a
// slices 2b-ii/2b-iii) — the bare cascade, entered at `entry`, with nothing in front of it.
//
// It serves `int.compare` AND `bool.compare` (the installer states why bool's graph IS int's). `float`
// cannot use it: IEEE `<`/`>` are false for every NaN pair, so `buildFloatCompare` enters the SAME cascade
// from a NaN preamble instead.
function buildOrderingCompare(module StdModule, name ByteArray, operandType StdType, ordering EnumLayout)
	var func = createTwoOperandImpl(name, operandType: operandType, resultType: builtinProtocolResultStdType(OrderingTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var lessBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"less")
	var notLessBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notLess")
	var greaterBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"greater")
	var equalBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"equal")

	emitTypedParams(module, block: entry, paramTypes: func.paramTypes)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId
	let other = 1 as ValueId

	emitOrderedCascade(module, head: entry, a: receiver, b: other, operandType: operandType, ordering: ordering, lessBlock: lessBlock, notLessBlock: notLessBlock, greaterBlock: greaterBlock, equalBlock: equalBlock, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildOrderingCompare'

// ⭐⭐ `float.compare(self, other) -> Ordering` (P1.7a slice 2b-iii) — a **TOTAL** order over f64, which IEEE
// `<`/`>`/`==` do not give: they answer FALSE for every NaN pair, so the bare cascade above would call a NaN
// `equalTo` everything. NaN is placed at the BOTTOM — below every non-NaN including `-inf` — and two NaNs
// compare `equalTo`. Both references agree exactly (`stdlib/PrimitiveExtensions.maxon:46-64`; the bootstrap
// documents it as Swift's `isTotallyOrdered(belowOrEqualTo:)`), and the canonical corpus pins all four NaN
// cases plus the signed-zero one.
//
// ⭐ **THE NaN TEST IS `x != x`** — the canonical IEEE spelling, true only when an operand is UNORDERED with
// itself. It is a `StdCmpPred.ne` over f64 and nothing more: x64 already builds a float `!=` as `setne OR
// setp` (and its branch-side `ordered`-block split), documented `unordered ⇒ != is TRUE`, and wasm's
// `f64.ne` carries the same semantics natively. A hand-rolled parity test here would be a second, weaker
// copy of a lowering the whole float suite already exercises.
//
// ⭐ **`-0.0` REACHES `equalTo` BY FALLING THROUGH, and there is deliberately no branch for it.** Under IEEE
// neither `-0.0 < 0.0` nor `-0.0 > 0.0` holds, so the cascade's final exit is already the right answer, and
// both references do exactly this. (`buildFloatHash` DOES need an explicit `-0.0` branch, and the difference
// is that IEEE settles this one for free while a HASH reads the bits, where the two zeros differ.)
//
// The `other != other` test is written TWICE, once per side of the `self` test, exactly as both references
// write it. Hoisting it would make it a VALUE two blocks read rather than a branch, which on x64 costs the
// `setne`+`setp`+`lea` materialization instead of a fused jump — a bigger body, to save a duplicated compare.
function buildFloatCompare(module StdModule, name ByteArray, ordering EnumLayout)
	var func = createTwoOperandImpl(name, operandType: StdType.f64, resultType: builtinProtocolResultStdType(OrderingTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var selfIsNanBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"selfIsNan")
	var selfNotNanBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"selfNotNan")
	var orderedBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ordered")
	var lessBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"less")
	var notLessBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notLess")
	var greaterBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"greater")
	var equalBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"equal")

	emitTypedParams(module, block: entry, paramTypes: func.paramTypes)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId
	let other = 1 as ValueId

	// The NaN preamble settles two of the three answers before any ordered comparison is meaningful, and
	// branches STRAIGHT INTO the cascade's own verdict blocks rather than minting a second `ret` of a tag
	// those blocks already return.
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: entry, a: receiver, b: receiver, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, operandType: StdType.f64, thenBlock: selfIsNanBlock, elseBlock: selfNotNanBlock, ids: ids)
	// `self` is NaN: both NaN ⇒ `equalTo`; else `self` is the bottom ⇒ `lessThan`.
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: selfIsNanBlock, a: other, b: other, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, operandType: StdType.f64, thenBlock: equalBlock, elseBlock: lessBlock, ids: ids)
	// `self` is not NaN: `other` NaN ⇒ `self` outranks the bottom ⇒ `greaterThan`; else both are ordered.
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: selfNotNanBlock, a: other, b: other, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, operandType: StdType.f64, thenBlock: greaterBlock, elseBlock: orderedBlock, ids: ids)

	// Both operands are known ordered here, so what remains IS `int.compare`'s body — the same callee, not a
	// second transcription of it.
	emitOrderedCascade(module, head: orderedBlock, a: receiver, b: other, operandType: StdType.f64, ordering: ordering, lessBlock: lessBlock, notLessBlock: notLessBlock, greaterBlock: greaterBlock, equalBlock: equalBlock, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildFloatCompare'

// ⭐⭐ **`<name>(self, other) -> Ordering` — THE LEXICOGRAPHIC BYTE COMPARE OF TWO FUSED BYTE RECORDS
// (A5m-c).** The corpus's `Character.compare` (`stdlib/Character.maxon:80-104`), verbatim as a rule: walk the
// bytes up to the SHORTER length, and the first differing byte decides; if every byte agrees, the SHORTER
// record is `lessThan` and equal lengths are `equalTo`. Both operands are BORROWED — it reads `buffer@0`/
// `length@8` and walks the bytes, freeing and allocating nothing — so it is safe on an `.rdata` literal and
// on an owned temporary alike.
//
// ⭐ **PARAMETERIZED BY NAME so ONE graph serves TWO symbols** — `buildStrEq`'s arrangement and taken for its
// reason. It is emitted under `__char_cmp` for the `<`/`>`/`<=`/`>=` OPERATOR path
// (`GraphemeRuntime.installGraphemeRuntime`) and under `Character.compare` for the `Comparable` WITNESS path
// (`installBuiltinConformanceRuntime`), because a witness reloc names the impl by
// `qualifiedMethodName("Character", "compare")`. Two `.text` symbols, one graph, so `a < b` and a
// `where T is Comparable` dispatch cannot come to order Characters differently.
//
// ⚠ **THE OPERATOR DOOR MAY NOT SIMPLY NAME `Character.compare`, AND THAT IS `ManagedHashName`'s ARGUMENT WITH
// ITS SECOND HALF MISSING.** `Character` IS reserved (`TypeResolution.isCompilerOwnedTypeName`), so no user
// can mint that symbol — but unlike `String`, a source-written `Character.<member>(…)` reaches
// `SemanticCheck.validateCall` rather than a parser interception (MEASURED: `Character.fromCodepoint(65)` is
// a clean **E3004: call to undefined function 'Character.fromCodepoint'**). Admitting the qualifier to
// `IrInterface.isBuiltinConformanceImplName` would make `isCompilerInternalCallee` wave that call past E3004
// and turn a positioned diagnostic into a link failure. A `__`-prefixed name needs no such reservation.
//
// ⚠ **THE BYTE COMPARES ARE UNSIGNED, and they are unsigned because the LOAD is** — `StdType.u8` through
// `emitLoadByteIndexed` zero-extends into the machine word (`TargetDialect`: `movzx dest, byte [base+disp]`),
// so a lead byte `0xC3` arrives as 195 rather than as -61. Read signed, an ASCII-based cluster
// (`e` + COMBINING ACUTE, `65 CC 81`) would order ABOVE the precomposed `é` (`C3 A9`), which is the opposite
// of what the corpus and the oracle both answer. `emitByteRangeCompareLoop` could never have caught it: an
// equality walk gives the same verdict either way. `specs-shv2/character-type.md`'s
// `character-ordering-compares-bytes-unsigned` is the case that does.
//
// The loop carries `[i, minLen]` as the header's two phis — `emitDjb2HashOverByteRange`'s shape with the
// bound carried instead of an accumulator, because the bound is chosen by a BRANCH (the shorter of the two
// lengths) rather than computed. Both `argType`s are set explicitly: `retypeBlockArgs` runs in
// `lowerMaxonToStd`, which these post-pipeline functions never pass through.
export function buildByteRecordCompare(module StdModule, name ByteArray, ordering EnumLayout)
	var func = createTwoOperandImpl(name, operandType: StdType.i64, resultType: builtinProtocolResultStdType(OrderingTypeName))
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var selfShorter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"selfShorter")
	var otherShorter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"otherShorter")
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"header")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"body")
	var byteNotLess = module.addBlock(func, label: b"byteNotLess")
	var nextByte = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nextByte")
	var lengthTie = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lengthTie")
	var lenNotLess = module.addBlock(func, label: b"lenNotLess")
	var lessBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"less")
	var greaterBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"greater")
	var equalBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"equal")

	emitTypedParams(module, block: entry, paramTypes: func.paramTypes)
	let receiver = 0 as ValueId
	let other = 1 as ValueId

	// Every field the whole body reads is loaded ONCE in `entry`, which dominates every block below — the
	// two lengths are read again at the length tie, so re-loading them there would be a second read of a
	// record the caller may not have kept alive any more than this one word.
	let selfLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: receiver, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let otherLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: other, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let selfBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: receiver, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let otherBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: other, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: entry, a: selfLen, b: otherLen, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, operandType: StdType.i64, thenBlock: selfShorter, elseBlock: otherShorter, ids: ids)

	// The two arms exist only to hand the header its bound. `otherShorter` is reached when the lengths are
	// EQUAL too, and either length is the right bound there.
	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let minLen = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(minLen, argType: StdType.i64))

	let selfZero = emitConst(module, block: selfShorter, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(selfShorter, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	selfShorter.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(selfZero, second: selfLen)))

	let otherZero = emitConst(module, block: otherShorter, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(otherShorter, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	otherShorter.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(otherZero, second: otherLen)))

	// `i >= minLen` ⇒ every compared byte agreed, so only the lengths are left to separate them. Two empty
	// records take this edge immediately and land on `equalTo`.
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: header, a: i, b: minLen, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64, thenBlock: lengthTie, elseBlock: body, ids: ids)

	// The first differing byte ANSWERS; a tied byte falls through to the next index (which is why this is two
	// `emitOrderingTest`s rather than the shared cascade — see `emitOrderedCascade`).
	let selfByte = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: body, base: selfBuf, index: i, ids: ids)
	let otherByte = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: body, base: otherBuf, index: i, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: body, a: selfByte, b: otherByte, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, operandType: StdType.i64, thenBlock: lessBlock, elseBlock: byteNotLess, ids: ids)
	emitOrderingTest(module, block: byteNotLess, a: selfByte, b: otherByte, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, operandType: StdType.i64, thenBlock: greaterBlock, elseBlock: nextByte, ids: ids)

	let one = emitConst(module, block: nextByte, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iNext = emitBin(module, block: nextByte, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(nextByte, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	nextByte.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(iNext, second: minLen)))

	// The tiebreak IS the ordinary cascade over the two lengths — shorter first — and it owns the three
	// verdict blocks the byte loop branches into, so `lessThan` means one thing in this graph.
	emitOrderedCascade(module, head: lengthTie, a: selfLen, b: otherLen, operandType: StdType.i64, ordering: ordering, lessBlock: lessBlock, notLessBlock: lenNotLess, greaterBlock: greaterBlock, equalBlock: equalBlock, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildByteRecordCompare'

// `String.hash(self) -> HashValue` — djb2 over the String's bytes. `self` is a by-value String POINTER in
// arg-0 (the witness dispatch passes `self.item`, an opaque `T` field read, straight in — a `String` rides
// like a struct receiver). It BORROWS `self`: it reads `buffer@0`/`length@8` and walks the bytes, freeing
// nothing — so a hashed boxed String neither double-frees nor leaks (the container drops it once).
//
// `h = 5381`; for each byte `b`: `h = h * 33 + b`; return `h and 0xFFFFFFFF`. The mask is applied ONCE at the
// end: i64 multiply/add wrap mod 2^64, and `(x mod 2^64) mod 2^32 == x mod 2^32`, so masking only at the end
// yields the same low-32 djb2 as masking every iteration would — for a String of any length. The loop carries
// its index `i` and accumulator `h` as the header's two phis, the `buildStrEq` byte-loop shape with an
// accumulator added. Both phi `argType`s are set to i64 explicitly: `retypeBlockArgs` runs in
// `lowerMaxonToStd`, which these post-pipeline functions never pass through.
function buildStrHash(module StdModule, name ByteArray)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(builtinProtocolResultStdType(HashValueTypeName)), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let strPtr = 0 as ValueId

	// entry: load `buffer@0`/`length@8`, then the shared djb2 walk over those bytes.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	emitDjb2HashOverByteRange(module, func: func, cur: entry, buffer: buffer, len: length, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildStrHash'

// ⭐⭐ **THE WHOLE OF A djb2 BYTE HASH — the loop AND the masked return.** `h = 5381`, then `h = h * 33 + b`
// over `buffer[0..len)`, then `return h & 0xFFFFFFFF`. Emitted off `cur`, which it terminates into a fresh
// header; the exit block is its own.
//
// ⚠ **IT OWNS THE TAIL RATHER THAN HANDING THE ACCUMULATOR BACK, AND THAT IS A CODEGEN FACT RATHER THAN A
// STYLE ONE.** Block creation ORDER is emission order, which decides layout and therefore register
// assignment. Returning `(done, h)` for the caller to finish would make the caller mint `done` — before the
// loop's own two blocks, inverting the order `buildStrHash` had before this emitter was extracted. MEASURED:
// that inversion alone moved `String.hash`'s register allocation in **42 committed fragments**, for a body
// computing the identical djb2. Owning the tail costs nothing, because BOTH callers' tails were already the
// same line.
//
// ⚠ **SHARED FOR `emitByteRangeCompareLoop`'s REASON, one protocol over.** `String.hash`, `Character.hash`
// and `Array.hash` all fold raw bytes with djb2, and the algorithm is a CONTRACT between them and every
// container that hashes their values: a widened read or a changed multiplier reaching one and not another
// would leave two conformers hashing the same bytes differently, with nothing failing to compile.
//
// The loop carries `[i, h]` as the header's two phis — the `emitByteRangeCompareLoop` shape with an
// accumulator added. `len == 0` folds nothing and yields the bare seed (`""` -> 5381). Both `argType`s are
// set explicitly: `retypeBlockArgs` runs in `lowerMaxonToStd`, which these post-pipeline functions never
// pass through.
//
// ⚠ The low-32 mask is the CALLER's (`returnLow32Masked`) and deliberately not applied per iteration: i64
// multiply/add wrap mod 2^64 and `(x mod 2^64) mod 2^32 == x mod 2^32`, so one mask at the end is the same
// low-32 djb2 as a mask every round, over a range of any length.
//
// ⚠ The three block LABELS are the ones `buildStrHash` carried before the extraction, and they are kept
// deliberately: a label is emitted into every `jmp` a spec fragment records, so renaming them would rewrite
// **192 committed goldens** to say nothing about the code.
export function emitDjb2HashOverByteRange(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, buffer ValueId, len ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"header")
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"body")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	let hSeed = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: Djb2Seed, ids: ids)
	let iSeed = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// The loop's two carried phis, in the order `[i, h]` — the SAME order every incoming edge pushes below.
	let i = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let h = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(h, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(iSeed, second: hSeed)))

	// header: `i >= len` ⇒ every byte folded in ⇒ out to `done`; else fold `buffer[i]`.
	let atEnd = emitCmp(module, block: header, a: i, b: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(header, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: done.id, elseBlockId: body.id))

	// body: `h = h * 33 + buffer[i]`; `i = i + 1`; back-edge. `i`/`h` are the header phis (header dominates
	// body), so they are read directly.
	let curByte = emitLoadByteIndexed(module, block: body, base: buffer, index: i, ids: ids)
	let mult = emitConst(module, block: body, value: Djb2Multiplier, ids: ids)
	let hScaled = emitBin(module, block: body, a: h, b: mult, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let hNext = emitBin(module, block: body, a: hScaled, b: curByte, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let one = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iNext = emitBin(module, block: body, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	body.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: twoArgs(iNext, second: hNext)))

	// done: mask the accumulator to the low 32 bits and return it (`h` is the header phi, and header
	// dominates done).
	returnLow32Masked(module, block: done, value: h, ids: ids)
end 'emitDjb2HashOverByteRange'
